<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<preface xml:id="hks-preface">
  <title>Preface and conventions</title>
  <p>A Lagrangian fibration on a hyperkähler manifold has abelian varieties as its smooth fibres. Their polarizations and periods constrain the fibration, while singular fibres, monodromy, and the absence of a section affect its global geometry. These notes develop this geometry and explain the hyperkähler SYZ theorem: a nonzero nef line bundle of Beauville–Bogomolov square zero is semiample.</p>
  <p><xref ref="hks-part-fibrations"/> begins with isotropic line bundles and examples. It then treats fibre polarizations and variation, special Kähler geometry, singular fibres, torsors and dual fibrations, and the geometry of the base. Elliptic K3 surfaces and Beauville–Mukai systems supply examples throughout. The constructions on the smooth locus are distinguished from their extensions across the discriminant.</p>
  <p><xref ref="hks-part-syz"/> gives a continuous account of the semiampleness proof. A degeneration adapted to the specified isotropic class produces a special Lagrangian torus. Its cohomology class allows hyperkähler rotation to a holomorphic Lagrangian torus. A fibration theorem and semiampleness in families then return the construction to the original nef pair. The final chapter discusses nonvanishing and smoothing methods and consequences for deformation types.</p>
  <p>Local calculations and the deductions between the quoted results are proved here. The analytic existence theorem for special Lagrangian tori, global deformation theorems, classification of singular fibres, and cohomological support theorems are stated with their hypotheses and cited. Recent results are used at the versions specified in the bibliography. This includes the OpenAI manuscripts <xref ref="hks-bib-openai-syz"/> and <xref ref="hks-bib-openai-bases"/>, Engel–Mauri <xref ref="hks-bib-engel-mauri"/>, and the work cited in the chapters on singular fibres and torsors.</p>
  <p>The companion <url href="/notes/hyperkahler/hyperkahler-notes.html">Notes on hyperkähler geometry</url> develops the metric, BBF, deformation, Torelli, cone, and moduli-space results. We recall their precise conclusions where needed. Mixed Hodge theory and the decomposition theorem are cited as external theorems; their applications here are explained without interrupting the geometric arguments with partial accounts of those theories.</p>
  <p xml:id="hks-conventions">An IHS manifold is a compact simply connected Kähler manifold <m>X</m> with <m>H^0(X,\Omega_X^2)=\C\sigma</m>, where <m>\sigma</m> is everywhere nondegenerate. For compact manifolds, <em>hyperkähler</em> means IHS throughout this note. A hyperkähler metric on a local model is understood in the differential-geometric sense. We write <m>\dim X=2n</m>; dimension means complex dimension unless real dimension is specified. Unlabelled cohomology groups have rational coefficients. The same letter denotes a closed form and its de Rham class when an integral or a pairing only depends on the class.</p>
  <p>The BBF bilinear form is <m>q_X(-,-)</m>, with <m>q_X(\alpha)=q_X(\alpha,\alpha)</m>, normalized to be primitive integral and positive on Kähler classes. The Fujiki constant <m>c_X&gt;0</m> is defined by <m>\int_X\alpha^{2n}=c_Xq_X(\alpha)^n</m>. A line bundle specified in the semiampleness problem is denoted by <m>L</m>, its first Chern class by <m>\lambda</m>, and the positive primitive integral generator of its ray by <m>e</m>, so that <m>\lambda=m e</m> for an integer <m>m&gt;0</m>.</p>
  <p>For a hyperkähler metric <m>g</m> and a quaternionic triple <m>I,J,K</m>, we use <m>IJ=K</m> and <m>\omega_A(u,v)=g(Au,v)</m>. Thus <m>\sigma_I=\omega_J+i\omega_K</m> and <m>\sigma_J=\omega_K+i\omega_I</m>. The normalized holomorphic volume form for <m>J</m> is <m>\Omega_J=\sigma_J^n/n!</m>. Special Lagrangian submanifolds carry the orientation determined by their positive calibration. For unipotent monodromy <m>T</m>, we set <m>N=\log T</m>.</p>
</preface>
